      "input": "ops = \"op\", vals = [5924, -7733, 7455]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(ops: string, vals: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Get the brute force right first: one array as the stack, rescanned from bottom to top every time a minimum is requested.",
    "The minimum of the stack changes only on a push or a pop, and only in a predictable way. What extra structure could hold that value for every stack height?",
    "Keep a parallel stack whose entry at height h is the minimum of the bottom h elements: a push asserts min(previous entry, value), and a pop removes one entry from both stacks. Read the two inputs together over the shorter of their lengths."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps one array as the stack and answers every `'m'` query by walking the whole stack from bottom to top to find the smallest value. That is correct, but each minimum query costs O(n), so n steps cost O(n\u00b2). The optimal version keeps a second stack of running minima, aligned by height with the main stack: pushing a value v asserts min(min_stack.top, v) onto the min stack, and popping removes one entry from both stacks. The entry at height h of the min stack is the minimum of the bottom h elements, so both `'t'` and `'m'` become a single array read. Both implementations drive the loop over the steps that exist, which is the shorter of the two input lengths.",
    "why_optimal": "Every step character that exists must be examined at least once, so O(n) time is the floor, and the algorithm hits it: every push and pop touches a constant number of stack entries. The extra structure costs O(n) space, the same order as the stack itself. No constant-time minimum lookup after an arbitrary pop is possible without remembering the running minima, because the minimum of the remaining stack depends on all of its elements and not just on the top.",
    "pitfalls": "Reading `'m'` on an empty stack must produce -1 rather than crash, and popping an empty stack is a no-op, so the pop and read branches both need a length check. The two inputs are separate arrays and are not guaranteed to be the same length, so the step count has to come from the shorter one: indexing `vals[i]` for a step that the value array does not reach raises an IndexError in Python and pushes `undefined` in JavaScript, and the two languages then disagree immediately. A common bug is seeding the running minimum from a sentinel such as 0 instead of from the pushed value itself, which reports 0 for a stack of only negative values. Authors also forget to pop the min stack alongside the main stack, which desynchronises the two heights and leaves stale minima behind."
  },
